{"about":"Genetic events: documented changes in who a population descends from. Each event states what its own shape means, because they are not the same kind of shape.","events":[{"id":"g_bottleneck","label":"The male-line bottleneck — where it was measured","from":8000,"to":4000,"ramp":600,"fade":1200,"tier":"solid","tierNote":"the bottleneck; its CAUSE is contested","colour":"#a98fc0","shapeMeans":"A SAMPLING FOOTPRINT — one halo per sampled population, not a territory.","note":"**THE BOTTLENECK IS WELL SUPPORTED. THE CAUSE IS NOT.** Across the mid-Holocene the number of men leaving male-line descendants collapsed, while the number of women doing so did not. Karmin and colleagues read it from 456 whole Y-chromosome sequences and date the abstract's “second strong bottleneck” to “the last 10 ky”, with the Results reporting “a reduction at around 8–4 kya”. The mitochondrial curve over the same window rises. This is not a shortage of men: it is a collapse in the number of men who became anybody's ancestor.\n\n**Why it happened is argued, not settled.** Karmin's own suggestion is cultural — a change in the variance of male reproductive success. Zeng, Aw and Feldman (2018) propose patrilineal kin groups competing, and whole clans dying out together, so that a Y lineage rises or falls with the group carrying it. That paper is a mathematical model tested against the existing genetic record, not new genetic data, and it addresses “5000–7000 years ago” — a narrower window than the evidence paper it explains. Treat the shape as established and the mechanism as an open question.\n\n**THE SHAPE HERE IS A SAMPLING FOOTPRINT.** It is drawn only where Karmin et al. actually sampled: one halo around each of the 128 published population coordinates behind the 320 samples in their nine geographically explicit regions. There is deliberately no outline joining them, because an outline asserts that the ground between two samples was sampled.\n\nSo **the whole of the Americas is three villages in north-western Argentina** — Calchaquí, Colla and Wichí, 13 samples. Oceania is two villages in highland New Guinea, 6 samples. Africa is nine populations between Nigeria and Tanzania, 26 samples, with nothing in the north and nothing in the south. Australia is absent entirely: the study's two Australian samples were explicitly not used in its geographically explicit analyses. Europe, by contrast, is 34 populations and 81 samples.\n\n**Where this layer is blank, the finding has not been tested there — which is not the same as the finding being false there.**\n\nKarmin, M. et al. (2015). *Genome Research* 25, 459–466.","polys":[[[[2.9,16.303],[3.768,16.104],[4.464,15.549],[4.85,14.746],[4.85,13.854],[4.464,13.051],[3.768,12.496],[2.9,12.297],[2.032,12.496],[1.336,13.051],[0.95,13.854],[0.95,14.746],[1.336,15.549],[2.032,16.104]]],[[[2.9,16.303],[3.768,16.104],[4.464,15.549],[4.85,14.746],[4.85,13.854],[4.464,13.051],[3.768,12.496],[2.9,12.297],[2.032,12.496],[1.336,13.051],[0.95,13.854],[0.95,14.746],[1.336,15.549],[2.032,16.104]]],[[[2.9,16.303],[3.768,16.104],[4.464,15.549],[4.85,14.746],[4.85,13.854],[4.464,13.051],[3.768,12.496],[2.9,12.297],[2.032,12.496],[1.336,13.051],[0.95,13.854],[0.95,14.746],[1.336,15.549],[2.032,16.104]]],[[[0.9,17.9],[1.768,17.702],[2.464,17.147],[2.85,16.345],[2.85,15.455],[2.464,14.653],[1.768,14.098],[0.9,13.9],[0.032,14.098],[-0.664,14.653],[-1.05,15.455],[-1.05,16.345],[-0.664,17.147],[0.032,17.702]]],[[[-3.7,37.104],[-2.832,36.906],[-2.136,36.35],[-1.75,35.546],[-1.75,34.654],[-2.136,33.85],[-2.832,33.294],[-3.7,33.096],[-4.568,33.294],[-5.264,33.85],[-5.65,34.654],[-5.65,35.546],[-5.264,36.35],[-4.568,36.906]]],[[[0.6,36.8],[1.468,36.602],[2.164,36.047],[2.55,35.245],[2.55,34.355],[2.164,33.553],[1.468,32.998],[0.6,32.8],[-0.268,32.998],[-0.964,33.553],[-1.35,34.355],[-1.35,35.245],[-0.964,36.047],[-0.268,36.602]]],[[[-1.3,37.301],[-0.432,37.102],[0.264,36.547],[0.65,35.745],[0.65,34.855],[0.264,34.053],[-0.432,33.498]