8.907],[46.436,89.736],[46.05,90.935],[46.05,92.265],[46.436,93.464],[47.132,94.293]]],[[[-25.1,-63.991],[-24.232,-64.21],[-23.536,-64.823],[-23.15,-65.709],[-23.15,-66.691],[-23.536,-67.577],[-24.232,-68.19],[-25.1,-68.409],[-25.968,-68.19],[-26.664,-67.577],[-27.05,-66.691],[-27.05,-65.709],[-26.664,-64.823],[-25.968,-64.21]]],[[[-24.2,-64.107],[-23.332,-64.324],[-22.636,-64.933],[-22.25,-65.812],[-22.25,-66.788],[-22.636,-67.667],[-23.332,-68.276],[-24.2,-68.493],[-25.068,-68.276],[-25.764,-67.667],[-26.15,-66.788],[-26.15,-65.812],[-25.764,-64.933],[-25.068,-64.324]]],[[[-23.2,-61.924],[-22.332,-62.14],[-21.636,-62.743],[-21.25,-63.616],[-21.25,-64.584],[-21.636,-65.457],[-22.332,-66.06],[-23.2,-66.276],[-24.068,-66.06],[-24.764,-65.457],[-25.15,-64.584],[-25.15,-63.616],[-24.764,-62.743],[-24.068,-62.14]]]]},{"id":"g_beaker","label":"The Beaker turnover — 90% of Britain’s gene pool","from":4476,"to":4026,"ramp":120,"fade":500,"tier":"solid","tierNote":"the replacement; its MECHANISM is contested and the paper names none","colour":"#c98f6f","shapeMeans":"THE GROUND THE FINDING IS ABOUT — the island where the 90% applies.","note":"**NINE PEOPLE IN TEN, REPLACED.** Beaker pottery spreads across western and central Europe from about 2750 BCE. It reaches Britain around 2450 BCE, and what follows is the sharpest population turnover anywhere in the European ancient-DNA record. Olalde and colleagues sequenced 400 Neolithic, Copper Age and Bronze Age Europeans, 226 of them buried with Beaker-complex artefacts.\n\n**The figure, in the paper's own words.** The abstract: the spread of the Beaker complex “was associated with the replacement of approximately 90% of Britain's gene pool within a few hundred years”. The body is more precise and reaches further forward: “a minimum of **90±2% local population turnover by the Middle Bronze Age (~1500–1000 BCE)**”. The authors add their own caveat, and it is kept here: “the exact turnover rate and its geographic pattern will be refined with more ancient samples.” The window this layer draws, 2450–2000 BCE, is the paper's own for the first centuries after contact.\n\n**IT TRAVELLED AS AN IDEA, THEN AS PEOPLE.** This is the part that makes it more than a migration story. Between Iberia and central Europe the pottery moved and the ancestry did not: the authors found “limited genetic affinity” between Beaker-associated people in the two regions and “thus exclude migration as an important mechanism of spread between these two regions”. From central Europe onward it is the other way round — “migration had a key role”, carrying steppe-related ancestry into Britain and continuing the east-to-west expansion of the centuries before. **The same archaeological culture spread one way in one place and the other way in another**, which is why pots have never been a safe proxy for people.\n\n**THE REPLACEMENT IS WELL SUPPORTED. WHY IT HAPPENED IS NOT, AND THE PAPER SAYS SO BY SAYING NOTHING.** Olalde et al. name no mechanism. They write that the results “motivate further archaeological research to identify the changes in social organization, technology, subsistence, climate, population sizes”, and list pathogen exposure among the things to look at. Violence, disease and social advantage are all live hypotheses and none of them is this paper's finding. **Do not read the 90% as a massacre; read it as an unexplained replacement.**\n\n**AND STONEHENGE WAS ALREADY STANDING.** This is two dated facts placed side by side, not a claim either paper makes — Olalde does not mention Stonehenge once. Parker Pearson and colleagues date the **sarsen circle and the trilithons to 2620–2480 BC**, and the monument's first phase to 3015–2935 cal BC. Their paper notes, independently, “the presence of Beaker pottery in Britain before 2500 BC”. So the great stones went up, and within a few generations **the people who raised them were largely replaced by people who did not build it.** The monument outlasted its builders' descendants.\n\n**AND IBERIA WAS GOING THE SAME WAY AT THE SAME TIME.** This map already carries the Iberian male-line turnover — close to 100% of that peninsula's Y chromosomes replaced by R1b-M269 between 2500 and 2000 BCE, against about 40% of its overall ancestry. The two are contemporary, both carry steppe-related ancestry, and they were reported by the same lead author a year apart: Olalde et al. 2018 for Britain, Olalde et al. 2019 for Iberia. **One expansion, arriving at both ends of Europe within a century.**\n\n**THE SHAPE HERE IS THE GROUND THE FINDING IS ABOUT** — the island of Great Britain, where the 90% applies — not a sampling footprint. Compare the bottleneck layer, where the shape means the opposite thing and says so.\n\nOlalde, I. et al. (2018). *Nature* 555, 190–196. Stonehenge phases: Parker Pearson, M. et al. (2007). *Antiquity* 81, 617–639.","polys":[[[[51.727,-2.465],[51.727,-2.465],[51.727,-2.464],[51.727,-2.465]]],[[[58.635,-3.053],[58.434,-3.101],[58.321,-3.212],[57.937,-4.004],[57.965,-4.085],[57.87,-4.031],[57.909,-4.393],[57.819,-3.857],[57.706,-4.028],[57.681,-4.295],[57.579,-4.429],[57.664,-4.086],[57.553,-4.263],[57.493,-4.195],[57.579,-4.068],[57.708,-3.403],[57.702,-2.074],[57.612,-1.867],[57.474,-1.781],[56.562,-2.593],[56.367,-3.324],[56.354,-3.294],[56.354,-3.252],[56.398,-2.885],[56.318,-2.653],[56.253,-2.674],[56.028,-3.362],[56.108,-3.838],[56.011,-3.656],[55.948,-3.078],[56.027,-2.599],[55.903,-2.147],[55.57,-1.655],[55.158,-1.518],[54.627,-1.204],[54.504,-0.671],[54.118,-0.084],[54.024,-0.22],[53.622,0.105],[53.737,-0.261],[53.733,-0.637],[53.702,-0.727],[53.692,-0.294],[53.335,0.271],[53.16,0.356],[52.888,0.01],[52.77,0.384],[52.967,0.559],[52.959,1.056],[52.754,1.657],[52.469,1.747],[52.12,1.591],[51.957,1.317],[51.996,1.264],[52.03,1.158],[51.978,1.275],[51.955,1.069],[51.917,1.259],[51.879,1.199],[51.845,1.274],[51.803,1.188],[51.845,0.98],[51.721,0.698],[51.689,0.898],[51.571,0.891],[51.507,0.456],[51.454,0.384],[51.468,0.6],[5